# The Problem

Language isn't precise - it always needs definition.
To formalize positions, find commonality is a problem.

Social Media as we know it from Instagram, TikTok etc creates bubbles or fronts, hate.


# Target

An argumentation graph platform to come to defined positions on a specific topic.
What leads me to an opinion.
A position based on beliefs and best case on facts - real facts (studies, real peer reviewed studies with a proper setup) or valid authorities which act with integrity and moral standards.
Show dependencies between Theses.
We want a grammar solving the issue to come to coexisting, defined and valid positions.

Truth is even with facts relative to subjects and circumstances.
We want to find as much consent on topics and show disagreement with respect.
Other positions are ok.
But qualify yourself.
Opinion without a proper argumentation line weighs simply less in a discourse.

We search commonalities (can be several) to a specific Thesis.


# Base Mechanics of the Tool/platform

## Thesis
- A Thesis is the central entity. Someone proposes a claim, question or subject to discuss.
- If you enter a Thesis it's probably already there but tackled differently - start from there and do a link.
- Theses can depend on or relate to other Theses (graph edges).
- A Thesis is never "closed" - it evolves. History is tracked as a changelog.
- Author can edit their own Thesis (title, description, categories).
- Admin can **archive** (not delete) a Thesis. Archived Theses stay reachable via link but are removed from trending/top lists.
- Every Thesis carries a **lifecycle state** (seedling → discussed → contested → crystallized → faded → dormant) computed deterministically from age, votes, arguments and last activity. See `.data.skill` for thresholds.
- Theses in "hot" states (seedling / discussed / contested / crystallized) are surfaced in listings. Faded / dormant theses stay reachable but are quiet by default.

## Arguments
- Arguments support or reject a Thesis (**stance** field, mandatory: `support` | `reject`).
- Arguments are votable (support | reject | neutral).
- Arguments carry evidence attributes (evidence_type + optional source_url).
- Arguments per Thesis: Fibonacci-capped, up to 8 per stance (adjustable down to 1 via complexity slider).
- **No reply chains.** Instead of replying to an argument, users **fork** it: copy the content, adapt it, and submit as a new *variant* in the same stance. Root + variants form one *argument group*.
- A group renders as ONE card: leading variant shown, expandable into a radio-list picker. One vote per group; picking a variant moves your vote there.
- Same principle will later apply to Thesis (fork instead of edit-war).

## Voting
- Support | Reject | Neutral, for both Theses and Arguments.
- Each anonymous user votes once. Casting the same vote again retracts it (toggle).
- For an argument GROUP (a fork family), a user has ONE vote across all variants — voting a variant moves the vote off its siblings.
- Reading and base voting (weight 1) are always free. Only extra vote *weight* costs budget.
- Vote direction is treated neutrally for visibility (support == reject). We do not assume reject = "bad"; a contra-argument can be up-voted for quality. The majority sorts meaning.

## Weighting
- A vote's weight walks a Fibonacci ladder: **1 → 2 → 3 → 5 → 8** (then resets). A strong opinion costs disproportionately more, which dampens weight-stacking abuse.
- Beyond raw votes, theses/arguments carry qualitative weight from:
  - Linked evidence (quality and quantity)
  - Chain of reasoning (logical coherence)
  - Peer endorsement (others vouch for structure, not conclusion)
- Unqualified opinions are visible but carry less weight.

## Input Budget (Fibonacci-flavoured)
- Participation is a currency, enforced **server-side** (client store is only an optimistic mirror). Reset at UTC midnight:
  - **Thesis**: 8 new theses / day
  - **Support argument**: 8 / day
  - **Reject argument**: 8 / day
  - **Vote weight pool**: 21 extra weight-points / day (base weight-1 votes are free)
- Reading, exploring and base voting are free.
- Editing your own thesis/argument does **not** cost budget.
- Forking an argument spends the respective stance bucket (it is a new variant).
- A freshly-minted identity may cast base votes only for its first minute (Sybil dampener) — no weighted votes until the identity matures.
- This forces thoughtfulness and naturally limits trolling.
- Budget is generous - it's a nudge, not a gate.
- The point: input must have value. This is not a comment section.

## Germination (letting the good keim)
- Young theses/arguments get a decaying visibility bonus (peak 8, ~1.5-day half-life) on trending / top ranking.
- This is the structural nudge so a good NEW minority position can sprout beside an established majority — even if the current majority is "wrong", better ideas get a fair window to grow. Direction-neutral: it rewards freshness, not a side.

## Categories
- Every Thesis has 1..n categories (e.g. education, policy, health, ...).
- Categories are dynamic strings, managed centrally by an admin (default categories are provided; admin can add/remove).
- On the landing page, categories are visualized as **tiles** whose size scales with the number of theses in that category (drill-down).

## Commonality Search
- The platform actively surfaces where Theses overlap.
- Consent is highlighted. Disagreement is shown with respect.
- Similarity detection via local small LLM / embedding-based clustering (later phase).

## Activity Visualization
- **Streamgraph** (pastel colors, symmetric around centerline) showing:
  - Support votes (green)
  - Creates (theses + arguments, purple)
  - Neutral votes (gray)
  - Reject votes (red)
- Rolling 5-day average for smooth curves.
- Time range: last 12 weeks (~84 days).
- Rendered in the right sidebar; context-aware:
  - Platform-wide on Trending/Top/My pages
  - Per-thesis on Thesis-detail page

## Heat Indicator
- Every ThesisCard shows a colored left-border indicating relative 24h activity:
  - **hot** (orange): ratio >= 1.5x platform average
  - **warm** (yellow): ratio >= 0.75x average
  - **cool** (light blue): some activity, below average
  - **cold** (blue): no 24h activity

## Complexity Slider
- Discrete **Fibonacci** steps (not linear) so choices feel natural and help people focus instead of drowning in a continuum.
  - Theses: 3 · 5 · 8 · 13 · 21 · 34 · 55
  - Arguments (per stance): 1 · 2 · 3 · 5 · 8
  - Related: 3 · 5 · 8 · 13
- One slider index drives all three dimensions in lockstep.
- Simplified language mode available (later).
- TL;DR summaries generated from the argumentation structure (later).
- Affects both the list view and the graph view.
- Present in left sidebar on all views (except settings).


# What it is not

It's not an exposure of the vulnerable.
It's not a fighting place (only inside arguments).
It's not a comment section.


# Ethics

We respect each other as long as we respect the rules.
Good faith assumption.
Separation of person from position.


# Rules

1. The integrity of a being is untouchable. Always.
2. Separate the person from the position. Always.
3. Good faith. Assume it. Earn it.
4. Minorities deserve a voice and a safe space, but majority is majority.
5. Trolls get marked kindly first, firmly later. Repeated disturbance leads to ignore or exclusion.
6. Qualify yourself. Bring reasoning, bring evidence, bring nuance.


# Challenges

There are people who want to disturb.
Claim the right position.
Change and erode definition and meaning of words - this needs to be shown.
The rule framework needs to hold.
We need to prevent bots or several accounts.
Theses will repeat over time and maybe adjust - history tracked via changelog.
We limit input via budget (not the mistake of endless comment sections).
It needs a UID, but we want full anonymity (no visible username, only arguments count).


# Implementation

## MVP (Phase 1) - current
- Single instance, **SQLite** (`.data/quappe.db`, better-sqlite3, WAL), seeded with ~200 example theses.
- Core: Thesis CRUD (create, edit, archive), Arguments with stance + fork variants, Voting (toggle + Fibonacci weight), Category drill-down, Fibonacci Complexity Slider, server-enforced Input Budget (3 stance buckets + weight pool), Activity Streamgraph, Heat Indicator, About page.
- **Lifecycle model**: theses transition through seedling → discussed → contested → crystallized → faded → dormant deterministically; tier is derived from `lifecycle_state`.
- **Quality score** rewards discourse depth + evidence + engagement over mere agreement; `/top` includes contested theses; a germination boost lets fresh positions surface.
- **Identity**: anonymous, server-minted **JWT** (HS256) in an httpOnly cookie. `iat`/`exp` claims enable Sybil heuristics (fresh identities can't cast weighted votes for the first minute). Body `user_id`/`author_id` fields are ignored — only the verified token counts.
- **Abuse guards**: server-side daily budget enforcement, per-IP + per-user token-bucket rate limits, length caps, body-size cap.

## Phase 2
- Persistent DB → managed Postgres or similar (SQLite already covers single-instance).
- Auth (magic link) on top of the existing JWT identity; API keys; configurable rate limits.
- Multi-user with anonymous identity.
- RBAC roles: user | qualified user | moderator | admin.
- Stronger Sybil resistance (proof-of-work / attestation), similarity detection via local LLM.

## Phase 3
- Scale: API backbone for mobile apps.
- Regional instances.
- SSO for enterprise contexts.
- Server-side event log / changelog persistence.


# Design decisions parked for later (rationale kept so we don't re-litigate)

## No Thesis forking — use relations → revision → merge instead
Argument forks converge (one card, one vote per group, best wording wins).
Thesis forks would DIVERGE: a thesis is a container for a whole discourse
(votes + arguments), so forking it fragments the very consensus the platform
exists to find. 40 people agreeing across 8 near-identical thesis variants =
nobody reaches the 20-vote crystallisation threshold. That inverts the mission.

Also: a "forked thesis" is indistinguishable from a "new, related thesis" — and
we already model that with `related_thesis_ids` (graph edges). So the answer is
not a fork tree but a stronger relation graph, maturing in three stages:

1. **Relations first** — make "streams" on a topic visible. On create, when the
   similarity search finds a near-duplicate, actively offer "link as related
   thesis?". Visualise related theses as a cluster/stream. This is the data
   groundwork everything else builds on.
2. **Revision (owner cycle)** — the thesis owner can revise wording in place;
   history tracked as a changelog. Convergent answer to fragmentation: the
   discourse stays in ONE container, the phrasing just improves. (Needs
   changelog persistence — Phase 2/3.)
3. **Merge (active consensus work)** — merge several related theses; participants
   are notified via the update feed and re-position themselves; the merge event
   spikes activity/heat/germination so the topic resurfaces and pulls new
   people in. Most powerful, most complex (merge semantics for votes/arguments,
   re-positioning flow, abuse potential where someone merges strategically to
   dilute an inconvenient thesis). Phase 3.

Reason it's real-life-honest: there is no single canonical phrasing of a topic;
related, coexisting discourses are natural. We embrace that via a network of
related theses, NOT a genealogy of copies.

## Meta / science reporting (later)
Lifecycle, rules, quality metrics, heat and the activity streamgraph are, for
users, primarily about *transparency*. Later they double as a data source for
meta-reporting and external scientific correlation (e.g. how consensus forms,
which evidence types move opinion). Keep the derived metrics clean and
timestamped (ISO-8601) so they are analysable after the fact.

## Quappe as a platform (north star, not a now-todo)
The long-term shape is several independent building blocks around ONE API
contract, which is what makes the `quappe-org` umbrella meaningful:

- **quappe-service** — the API + SQLite/DB + all domain logic (the single source
  of truth). Everything else is a consumer.
- **quappe-web** — a presentation-only frontend that consumes the API. No domain
  logic in the client; it renders and calls endpoints. This is what allows
  multiple frontends and 3rd-party clients (the API already exists).
- **quappe-monitoring** (ops) and **quappe-analytics** (meta/science reporting) —
  further consumers of the same API / event stream.

Why NOT split the repo yet: today it is a single developer on an MVP. SvelteKit
already separates server (load, form actions, API routes) from client, and we
*already* have an API. A physical split (service/web) only pays off once a
second frontend or a second team/deployment exists — until then it doubles
deployment, CI and type-sync for no current benefit.

How we stay split-ready without splitting: the UI consumes ONLY the API — no
component imports `data.ts` or server DB modules directly (see technical rules
in CLAUDE.md). That keeps the eventual split a clean cut, not open-heart surgery.
